Tilapia is a widely farmed freshwater fish valued for its mild flavor, high protein content, and relatively affordable production. It is marketed as whole fish, fresh or frozen fillets, and processed seafood products for retail and food-service customers. Its versatility makes it a popular alternative to other whitefish across a range of culinary applications.

The industry is evolving as producers invest in aquaculture efficiency, feed innovation, water management, and improved fish health practices. Processors are also expanding value-added product offerings, including ready-to-cook fillets and portion-controlled seafood. These developments create opportunities for producers to improve operating efficiency and respond to changing consumer preferences.

